---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CommandBlock from '../components/CommandBlock.astro';
import {href,repository} from '../lib/urls';
const steps=[['lab','The lab'],['prepare','The draw'],['clock','The clock'],['grade','The reboot'],['diagnose','The diagnosis'],['verify','The self-tests'],['uninstall','The cleanup']];
---
<Layout title="How it works" description="Follow the autorelabel pipeline: an isolated lab, a timed exam, a verified reboot, persistent-state grading and a clean uninstall." path="/how-it-works/">
<main id="content" class="container how-page">
  <div class="page-intro"><h1>The reboot<br/><em>is the point.</em></h1><p>Follow the work from your ISO to the score. Every step has a job.</p></div>
  <div class="pipeline-layout"><nav class="pipeline-nav" aria-label="Exam pipeline">{steps.map(([id,label],index)=><a class="toc-link" href={`#${id}`} data-pipeline-link><span>{String(index+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span>{label}</a>)}</nav>
  <div class="pipeline-content prose">
    <section id="lab" data-pipeline-step><h2>A box from your DVD.<br/>An isolated lab.</h2><p>Packer installs RHEL 10 from your ISO into a reusable Vagrant box. It is built once per hypervisor. No subscription is used and the box is never registered.</p><p>Vagrant creates the VMs with libvirt/KVM or VirtualBox. The utility server provides repositories, DNS, time, NFS and other services from the DVD. A lab network carries your work; a separate management network lets the grader reach the nodes.</p><p><a class="text-link" href={href('/docs/the-lab/')}>Inspect the lab reference ↗</a></p></section>
    <section id="prepare" data-pipeline-step><h2>The shape stays fixed.<br/>The details change.</h2><p>The blueprint draws 20 tasks: 6 easy, 8 medium, and 6 hard. It keeps the required spread across objective domains and gives more weight to objectives you have practised less recently.</p><p>Names, sizes, ports and passwords are filled in at random. The lab returns to a clean snapshot before task setup, then saves the exam starting point for a retake.</p><p>RHCSA starts with console work. RHCE starts with an empty Ansible project. You read the instructions before starting the clock.</p></section>
    <section id="clock" data-pipeline-step><h2>The clock runs<br/>outside the interface.</h2><p>A background proctor tracks time, warns you as the end approaches, and locks and grades the lab at zero. Closing the interface does not stop an exam.</p><p>With the default setting, time while the host sleeps, crashes or is off does not count. The next run offers to resume. A stricter wall clock is available in Settings.</p><p><a class="text-link" href={href('/docs/exam-screen/')}>Read the exam screen reference ↗</a></p></section>
    <section id="grade" data-pipeline-step><h2>Before.<br/>Reboot.<br/><em>After.</em></h2><ol><li><strong>Pre-reboot pass.</strong> Check the state you left behind. This pass earns no points.</li><li><strong>Verified reboot.</strong> Reboot every exam node, wait for it to go down and return with a new boot ID.</li><li><strong>Post-reboot pass.</strong> Run the checks again. This is the score that counts.</li></ol><p>A check that passed before the reboot and failed afterwards gets a persistence warning. A service that was started but never enabled is the familiar example.</p><p>Partial scoring gives a task the share of its points corresponding to the checks that passed. Strict scoring requires every check. Guard checks stop an untouched task from earning points just because you have not broken anything.</p><p><a class="text-link" href={href('/docs/grading-and-results/')}>Read the scoring and persistence rules ↗</a></p></section>
    <section id="diagnose" data-pipeline-step><h2>A missing node<br/>gets an explanation.</h2><p>A normal reboot can escalate to an ACPI power-button press and then a hard reset. If a node does not return, autorelabel reads its serial console.</p><p>It can distinguish emergency mode, a GRUB prompt, a kernel panic, a login prompt with broken SSH, and a boot that is taking too long. A node that is relabelling gets more time.</p><p>Grading records progress after every check. A host interruption resumes from the saved state.</p></section>
    <section id="verify" data-pipeline-step><h2>The tasks are checked<br/>before you see them.</h2><p>Every task is self-tested on real RHEL 10 VMs, on both hypervisors. An untouched lab must score zero. The reference solution and accepted alternatives must earn full points. The classic mistakes must lose the right points.</p><p>Checks examine the final state. They accept valid alternatives, including mounting by UUID or LABEL and configuring a connection with nmcli or keyfiles.</p><p><a class="text-link" href={`${repository}/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>Read the architecture ↗</a></p></section>
    <section id="uninstall" data-pipeline-step><h2>A ledger in.<br/>A clean way out.</h2><p>Every resource is recorded when it is created: VMs, networks, storage, boxes and directories. The uninstaller reads the ledger, so it removes only what autorelabel created.</p><p>A rescue copy lives in the lab storage folder. It is plain bash and works even if the repository or private Python environment is gone. Your own hypervisor tools and ISO remain unless you explicitly request ISO deletion.</p><CommandBlock id="pipeline-uninstall" label="Inspect the cleanup list" commands={['./autorelabel uninstall --dry-run']}/><p>autorelabel never runs sudo itself. The doctor prints host setup commands and their matching undo commands for you to run.</p><p><a class="text-link" href={href('/docs/files-and-uninstall/')}>Files, options and the rescue uninstaller ↗</a></p></section>
  </div></div>
</main>
</Layout>
